*The nominative plural undergoes [[lenition]] only if the noun ends with a slender consonant: {{lang|ga|cait bhacacha}} "lame cats". Otherwise, the adjective in the nominative plural does not lenite: {{lang|ga|táilliúirí bacacha}} "lame tailors".
*The long form of the genitive plural (e.g. {{lang|ga|bochta}}, {{lang|ga|bacacha}}, {{lang|ga|ciúine}}) is used when the noun has a strong plural, e.g. {{lang|ga|máithreacha bacacha}} "of lame mothers". The short form (e.g. {{lang|ga|bocht}}, {{lang|ga|bacach}}, {{lang|ga|ciúin}}) is used when the noun has a weak plural, e.g. {{lang|ga|cat bacach}} "of lame cats".
*The dative has the same form as the nominative.
*The vocative has the same form as the nominative except in the masculine singular of the 1st/2nd declension, where it has the same form as the genitive.

===Comparative===
Irish adjectives have a [[comparative]] form equivalent to the comparative and [[superlative]] in English. The comparative does not undergo inflexion and is the same as the feminine singular genitive in regular and many irregular adjectives.

